- How would you implement countTrailingZeros128 without 128-bit intrinsics?
- How would you adapt this for a language without unsigned 64-bit types?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to count 1-runs in a 64-bit word using bit-twiddling with no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Start mask n & ~(n << 1) identifies 1-run starts
- Parallel popcount technique to count bits
- Edge cases: all zeros, runs spanning MSB

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t countOneRuns64(uint64_t n){
  uint64_t starts = n & ~(n << 1);
  uint64_t x = starts;
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit operands?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based scan in terms of latency and pipeline efficiency.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of in-place bitboard style transforms, pure bitwise logic, and branchless design for a fixed-width data layout.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice permutation across 8x8 matrix
- Masked swaps and shifts to perform transposition in O(1)
- Endianness and layout nullified by a consistent row-major interpretation

## Code Example
```javascript
function transpose8x8(x) {
  // Use BigInt for 64-bit arithmetic in JS
  let t;
  const m1 = 0x5555555555555555n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & m1;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 1n);

  const m2 = 0x3333333333333333n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & m2;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 2n);

  const m4 = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & m4;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 4n);

  return x;
}
```

